Below is a comprehensive example of how to build a shopping cart component using React and Redux Toolkit for state management. This example assumes you're familiar with setting up a basic Redux project using Redux Toolkit. If you're not familiar, you may want to start with a tutorial on how to set up a Redux project, and then come back to this example.

First, ensure you have the basic projects set up. I'll assume you have:

- A basic React app set up.
- Redux Toolkit installed via npm or yarn: `npm i @reduxjs/toolkit` or `yarn add @reduxjs/toolkit`.
- A Redux store configured (using `configureStore` from `@reduxjs/toolkit`).

### Shopping Cart Data Model

Start by defining the data model for your shopping cart. For simplicity, let's assume we're managing a list of items where each item has a unique ID, name, price, and quantity. We'll also use a `cartSlice` to manage this data.

```javascript
// data/cartSlice.js
import { createSlice } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';

const initialState = {
  items: {},
  // totalCartValue: 0, // Optional, you might not need this at the slice level
};

const cartSlice = createSlice({
  name: 'cart',
  initialState,
  reducers: {
    addItem(state, action) {
      const existingItem = state.items[action.payload.id];
      if (existingItem) {
        existingItem.quantity++;
      } else {
        state.items[action.payload.id] = {...action.payload, quantity: 1 };
      }
    },
    removeItem(state, action) {
      const { id } = action.payload;
      if (state.items[id].quantity > 1) {
        state.items[id].quantity--;
      } else {
        delete state.items[id];
      }
    },
    clearCart(state) {
      state.items = {};
    },
  },
});

export default cartSlice.reducer;
```

