Here's the CartComponent that pairs with the cartSlice:

```jsx
import React from'react';
import { useSelector, useDispatch } from'react-redux';
import { addItem, removeItem } from './cartSlice';
import CartItem from './CartItem';

const CartComponent = () => {
  const cart = useSelector(state => state.cart);
  const dispatch = useDispatch();

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Shopping Cart</h1>
      {cart.items.length === 0 && <p>No items in cart.</p>}
      {cart.items.map(item => (
        <CartItem key={item.id} item={item} dispatch={dispatch} />
      ))}
      <button onClick={() => dispatch(removeItem(item.id))}>
        Remove Item
      </button>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch(addItem(item))}>
        Add Item
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

In this example, `CartItem` flips between "Remove" and "Add" buttons depending on whether the item's quantity indicator is above 0. I'd typically structure `cartSlice` to maintain quantities explicitly to avoid sketchy implementation details. (I assume quantity is one-to-many at the item level — store quantities in the item entity itself and use a separate quantity indicator display component.) I'd rather manage quantity across the client than relay quantities from the server on every update. I'd lean toward storing an item's quantity on the client until the server comes back and tells me what the new quantity should be. This caps the amount of work the server does while allowing the app to stay responsive — I'd implement a delta-sync update every time I receive a quantity update from the server. This lets me use an smartsync strategy (where the client is the authoritative source of quantity) without sacrificing cache consistency — when I get an update from the server, I just update that locally-authoritative quantity until the next update arrives.

